Data splitting and preprocessing

set.seed(403)
projects_split &lt;- initial_split(projects, prop = 0.8)
projects_train &lt;- training(projects_split)
projects_folds &lt;- vfold_cv(projects_train, v = 10)



projects_rec &lt;- recipe(opening_time ~ ., data = projects_train) |&gt;
  update_role(project, new_role = "id") |&gt;
  step_log(budget, n_operations) |&gt;
  step_other(arrondissement, theme, threshold = 0.02)



nothing survival-specific here, except the response is a Surv object

A model for censored regression


Regression

linear_reg() |&gt;
  set_engine("lm") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")


Censored regression

cox_spec &lt;- proportional_hazards() |&gt;
  set_engine("survival") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")




cox_fit &lt;- workflow(projects_rec, cox_spec) |&gt;
  fit(data = projects_train)



same code as for regression; only the mode and the response differ

Two new engines for random forests

ranger_spec &lt;- rand_forest(trees = 500) |&gt;
  set_engine("ranger") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")

rfsrc_spec &lt;- rand_forest(trees = 500) |&gt;
  set_engine("randomForestSRC") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")



switching model type or engine is one line
why these? they are fast
why random forests? generally work well out of the box
but also: why tree-based models? censored has quite a few of those!
censored smooths over engine quirks, e.g. randomForestSRC’s 0/1 status coding and pre-made Surv responses in recipes

A null model as a baseline

null_spec &lt;- null_model() |&gt;
  set_engine("survival") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")


Ignores all predictors: one Kaplan-Meier curve for everyone
Does your model beat doing nothing?

Faster tuning for boosted trees

boost_tree(trees = 200) |&gt;
  set_engine("mboost") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression") |&gt;
  fit(opening_time ~ ., data = projects_train) |&gt;
  multi_predict(new_data, type = "survival", eval_time = 12, trees = c(50, 100, 200))


Fit once, predict for many numbers of trees
tune_grid() uses it automatically



in the censored blog post example: about 3x faster
learn_rate is now tunable too

The same pipeline for every metric

pred |&gt;
  bind_cols(testing(projects_split)) |&gt;
  brier_survival(opening_time, .pred)
#&gt; # A tibble: 3 × 4
#&gt;   .metric        .estimator .eval_time .estimate
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;          &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 brier_survival standard           12    0.0480
#&gt; 2 brier_survival standard           24    0.158 
#&gt; 3 brier_survival standard           36    0.221

Setting up

projects &lt;- projects |&gt;
  mutate(opening_time = Surv(months, opened), .keep = "unused")

set.seed(403)
projects_split &lt;- initial_split(projects, prop = 0.8)
projects_train &lt;- training(projects_split)
projects_folds &lt;- vfold_cv(projects_train, v = 10)

projects_rec &lt;- recipe(opening_time ~ ., data = projects_train) |&gt;
  update_role(project, new_role = "id") |&gt;
  step_log(budget, n_operations) |&gt;
  step_other(arrondissement, theme, threshold = 0.02)

cox_spec &lt;- proportional_hazards() |&gt;
  set_engine("survival") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")

A null model as your true baseline

null_spec &lt;- null_model() |&gt;
  set_engine("survival") |&gt;
  set_mode("censored regression")


Ignores all predictors: one Kaplan-Meier curve for everyone
Does your model beat doing nothing?

The final model

best_id &lt;- rank_results(
  projects_res,
  rank_metric = "brier_survival_integrated"
) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 1) |&gt;
  pull(wflow_id)

final_fit &lt;- projects_res |&gt;
  extract_workflow(best_id) |&gt;
  last_fit(projects_split, metrics = projects_metrics, eval_time = 1:96)



OPTIONAL slide
rank by integrated Brier score, take the best workflow
last_fit(): fit on all of the training data, evaluate once on the test data
